      // [src_frobel_britannica, src_frobel_ghdi, src_frobel_commonplay, src_frobel_edinburgh | 1816-1852 | encyclopaedia + historical institute + trade article + university project] Britannica and the German Historical Institute document the institute founded in 1816 and kept going at Keilhau, followed by the Swiss work and the return to Germany in 1836 to devote himself to young children; the German Historical Institute and the Edinburgh project record the kindergarten and women's training courses continuing until the Prussian ban of 1851, which came a year before his death, and Community Playthings credits co-workers with keeping Keilhau going under pressure. A long line of one purpose through several ventures and a hostile decree, though the sources are brief and several are sympathetic.
