      // [src_bose_live, src_bose_scroll, src_bose_ijpp | 1894-1917 | press + review] He built his first apparatus with no laboratory and a tinsmith, enlisted Lord Rayleigh to communicate his papers to the Royal Society, obtained funding through Tagore and R. C. Dutt from the Maharaja of Tripura when official support ran out, and used his reputation to 'enlist more and more support' culminating in the Bose Institute of 1917. The review also calls his demands 'insatiable' and 'exasperating'. The funding networks are reported by two independent sources.
