    directoryVisible: true,
    sources: [{ id: "src_kg_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Kahlil Gibran (Q47737) -- verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-10-24): English label 'Kahlil Gibran', description 'Lebanese-American writer, poet, and painter (1883-1931)', Korean label '칼릴 지브란', born 6 January 1883, died 10 April 1931, image 'Kahlil Gibran 1913.jpg'. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q47737" }, { id: "src_kg_poets", kind: "institution", title: "Academy of American Poets (poets.org), 'Kahlil Gibran' biography page, read in full: born in Bsharri in Ottoman Syria (now Lebanon); art classes in Boston and a first public exhibit of drawings in 1904 championed by the photographer Fred Holland Day; study at the Academie Julian in Paris in 1908 with Mary Haskell as patron; move to New York in 1911; publisher Alfred Knopf; The Madman (1918) and The Prophet (1923), which was never reviewed by the New York Times and sold about twelve hundred copies in its first year but has sold more than ten million, making him among the best-selling poets in the world; Juan Cole's remark on its appeal in the 1960s; membership of the Arabic-language Pen League; nine books in Arabic and eight in English; Sand and Foam (1926). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE (with the Mahjar guide). Institutional, admiring, with some detail (the 1885 emigration date and the 'reportedly' Rodin remark) not relied on; family-loss, patron-relationship and health passages were not used.", url: "https://poets.org/poet/kahlil-gibran" }, { id: "src_kg_mahjar", kind: "institution", title: "Academy of American Poets, 'A Brief Guide to the Mahjar' (poets.org, staff, 30 March 2022), read in full: the Mahjar movement of Syrian and Lebanese writers in the Americas as a facet of Arabic Romanticism (nonconformity, love and spirituality, influence of Whitman); the New York Pen League (al-Rabitah al-Qalamiyyah) began to meet in 1916 and was officially founded in April 1920, with Gibran and Ameen Rihani as members and Nasib Arida a founder of the journal al-Funun in which many members published; the Pen League was the most significant Mahjar circle and New York the publishing centre; the page also notes that Gibran spoke out about the wartime famine in Mount Lebanon (political; not used). Institutional overview of a literary movement.", url: "https://poets.org/text/brief-guide-mahjar" }, { id: "src_kg_qnl", kind: "institution", title: "Hadia H. Elkhalifa, 'Arab-American Literature: Poets of Al-Mahjar, the Pen League and the Andalusian League', Qatar National Library blog, 28 November 2021, read in full: the diaspora poets combined Arab culture and life in the diaspora and moved against traditional restrictions; the New York Pen League, formed in 1920 with Gibran, Elia Abu Madi, Mikhail Naimy and Abd al-Masih Haddad as prominent members, aimed to promote the Arabic language and its literature and edited and published diaspora periodicals including the literary magazine al-Funun; its output used simple Arabic, new forms such as free poetry and symbolic writing; the society dissolved with Gibran's death. A national-library editorial seen from an Arab institutional perspective, partly summary.", url: "https://www.qnl.qa/en/blogs/12396" }, { id: "src_kg_bigthink", kind: "press", title: "Gus Mitchell, 'Meet the 3rd bestselling poet in world history', Big Think, 18 October 2023, read in full: The Prophet (Knopf, September 1923) sold its first printing of about twelve hundred copies within a month with little promotion, sales grew year on year, and it is described as the third-bestselling poet's book behind Shakespeare and Lao Tzu; Knopf's remark that he had not met five people who read Gibran; in Arabic he is held to be a crucial modern innovator and a bridge between classical and romantic sensibility, while Western academic and literary opinion treats his concerns and style as anti-modern, overwritten, sentimental (counter-voice); he trained at the Academie Julian in 1908, produced over 700 visual works, saw himself in the poet-artist line of William Blake, and drew on Christian, Islamic and Sufi, Buddhist and Hindu traditions, Blake, Nietzsche, Whitman and the Transcendentalists. Popular-intellectual magazine essay by one writer; its remarks on his drinking and his father were not used.", url: "https://bigthink.com/high-culture/kahlil-gibran/" }, { id: "src_kg_aljadid", kind: "press", title: "D. H. Melhem, 'Gibran's The Prophet Outside the Canon of American Literature', Al Jadid (Arab-American arts and culture magazine), read in full (first ~9,000 characters of 14,000): The Prophet has sold over nine million copies and was in its 136th printing in August 2001, yet Gibran continues to be rejected by mainstream American critics and is absent from the Norton and Heath anthologies, with Grape Leaves (1988) the first major American anthology to feature him; his English books The Madman and Jesus, The Son of Man were better received critically; the writer compares his illustrated, parable-and-prose-poem form to William Blake and finds the book experimental, set against the Eliot-Pound modernist aesthetic. Essay by an Arab-American poet and scholar; sympathetic to Gibran but critical of his exclusion; rest of the article not opened.", url: "https://aljadid.com/content/gibran%E2%80%99s-%E2%80%98-prophet%E2%80%99-outside-canon-american-literature" }, { id: "src_kg_fac", kind: "press", title: "'A Greater Beauty: The Drawings of Kahlil Gibran', Fine Art Connoisseur (exhibition notice for The Drawing Center, New York, through 3 September 2023), read in full: Gibran, best known as the author of The Prophet, viewed himself equally as an artist, producing paintings, watercolours, sketches, illustrations and book covers as a complement to his writing; the notice says he broke with the rigid conventions of traditional Arabic poetry and prose and that his non-sectarian approach combining Christianity, Islam, Buddhism and Jungian psychology was a revelation to Arabic-speaking and immigrant communities, and that his visual art was an idiosyncratic fusion of symbolist pantheism and spiritual mysticism; exhibition curated by Claire Gilman with Anneka Lenssen; works lent by the Telfair Museum and the Gibran Museum. Exhibition press notice, promotional.", url: "https://fineartconnoisseur.com/2023/07/a-greater-beauty-the-drawings-of-kahlil-gibran/" }, { id: "src_kg_commons", kind: "institution", title: "Wikimedia Commons file page 'Kahlil Gibran with Book MET DP114605.jpg' and the Metropolitan Museum of Art collection record for object 267551 (accession 33.43.151, F. Holland Day, 'Kahlil Gibran with Book', 1896, platinum print, Alfred Stieglitz Collection, 1933) -- checked for the portrait: Commons records artist F. Holland Day (American, 1864-1933), institution the Metropolitan Museum of Art, licence tag CC0 from a Metropolitan Museum upload, original 2847 x 3898 pixels, SHA-1 verified against the downloaded file; the Met's own API record lists the object as not flagged public domain, with no image and no rights statement. Also checked: 'Kahlil Gibran 1913.jpg' (unknown author, published in al-Funun in April 1913, 274 x 379 pixels).", url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kahlil_Gibran_with_Book_MET_DP114605.jpg" }],
