    sources: [{ id: "src_kc_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Kalpana Chawla (Q237879) -- verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-10-24): English label 'Kalpana Chawla', description 'American astronaut', Korean label '칼파나 촐라', born 17 March 1962, died 1 February 2003, citizenship United States and India. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q237879" }, { id: "src_kc_nasabio", kind: "institution", title: "NASA Johnson Space Center, 'Kalpana Chawla (Ph.D.), NASA Astronaut (Deceased)' biographical data sheet (May 2004, read in full): degrees from Punjab Engineering College (1982), the University of Texas (1984) and the University of Colorado (1988); research at NASA Ames on powered-lift computational fluid dynamics from 1988; vice president and research scientist at Overset Methods from 1993; selection as an astronaut candidate in December 1994; assignment as prime robotic arm operator on STS-87; later crew-systems and habitability lead; STS-87 and STS-107 flights totalling 30 days 14 hours 54 minutes; her pilot and flight-instructor ratings. CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE. Official institutional biography; no trait claims, and its passage on her death is not used for any row.", url: "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/chawla_kalpana.pdf" }, { id: "src_kc_nasacrew", kind: "institution", title: "NASA, 'Remembering Columbia: STS-107 crew biographies' page, Kalpana Chawla entry (read for the mission-role passage only): flight engineer and Mission Specialist 2 for STS-107, a Red Team member responsible with the commander for maneuvering Columbia in support of payload-bay experiments, and the list of experiments she worked with (including the Combustion Module with its water-mist and flame-ball experiments, protein crystal growth and biotechnology units). Official mission record; the accident material on the page was not used.", url: "https://www.nasa.gov/remembering-columbia-sts-107" }, { id: "src_kc_sts87", kind: "archive", title: "NASA Johnson Space Center, 'STS-87 Space Shuttle Mission Report' (read in the sections on mission summary, robotic arm operations and the Spartan payload): the 19 November to 5 December 1997 Columbia mission, the Spartan 201-04 deployment, a failed re-grapple attempt and the manual capture by the spacewalking crew a day later, and a statement that a formal investigation board was convened. Official mission record without individual attribution; the board's findings were not found and not read, so no row or card rests on the Spartan episode.", url: "https://ibiblio.org/apollo/Shuttle/Reports/Mission%20Reports/STS-87%20Space%20Shuttle%20Mission%20Report.pdf" }, { id: "src_kc_ntrs", kind: "archive", title: "Overset Methods, Inc., 'Overset Grid Applications on Distributed Memory MIMD Computers', progress report for NASA Grant NCC 2-806 to NASA Ames Research Center, 30 June 1994 (NASA Technical Reports Server NASA-CR-196081; Principal Investigator Dr. Kalpana Chawla; read through its abstract, objectives and structure): a study of implementing an overset-grid implicit flow solver on 128-node and 208-node Intel parallel computers, with performance data for two composite grid configurations. Research record co-authored with Sisira Weeratunga; read at abstract and outline level, not for the numerical detail.", url: "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19940031890/downloads/19940031890.pdf" }, { id: "src_kc_uta", kind: "institution", title: "University of Texas at Arlington College of Engineering, 'UTA Remembers Kalpana Chawla, Columbia Crew 15 Years Later', 1 February 2018 (read in full): her UTA master's degree in 1984 and research on a cross-flow fan propulsion system, her professor Don Wilson recalling her as 'highly-motivated' with 'a burning desire to excel', Astronaut Office chief Kent Rominger saying she was admired 'for her strive for perfection', and her work at Ames and Overset Methods. Alumni-office tribute with admiring recollections; counted as retrospective praise, not as independent behavioural evidence, and the presidential memorial quotation was not used.", url: "https://www.uta.edu/academics/schools-colleges/engineering/about/news/2018/02/01/02-chawla-15-years" }, { id: "src_kc_nsf", kind: "press", title: "NASASpaceFlight.com, 'STS-107: Remembering the accomplishments of the Columbia crew', 2013 (read in full, in the flight-day account): the red and blue crew shifts of the 16-day science mission, the failure of a vacuum pump in the water-mist experiment and the ground team's fix, and the statement that Kalpana Chawla offered to give up her off-duty time on flight day 13 to carry out the repair, which in the end took five astronauts and two days. Space-press retrospective built from NASA mission reports; the entry and accident passages were not used.", url: "https://www.nasaspaceflight.com/2013/02/sts-107-remembering-columbia-crew/" }, { id: "src_kc_commons", kind: "institution", title: "NASA Image and Video Library record KSC-02pd1127 ('Official portrait of astronaut Kalpana Chawla, mission specialist', 8 March 2002), NASA media usage guidelines and the Wikimedia Commons file page 'Kalpana Chawla, NASA photo portrait in orange suit.jpg' (live check 2026-10-24) -- checked for the portrait.", url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalpana_Chawla,_NASA_photo_portrait_in_orange_suit.jpg" }],
    rows: {
      // [src_kc_ntrs, src_kc_nasabio | 1988-1994 | NASA research record + NASA biography] She was principal investigator on a 1994 NASA grant study of a parallel overset-grid flow solver tested on 128-node and 208-node computers, after research at Ames on powered-lift flow simulation and a doctorate in aerospace engineering. Technically demanding research is documented at first hand, but the report is joint work read here at abstract and outline level, so a modest score at low confidence. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): first-pass 62 / 0.5 lowered to 58 / 0.45 because the report was read only at abstract and outline level and is co-authored.
      analytical_rigor: [58, 0.45, "d", "A"],
      // [src_kc_nsf, src_kc_nasacrew | 2003 | space-press flight-day account + NASA crew record] During the STS-107 science mission she offered to give up her off-duty time on flight day 13 to repair the water-mist combustion experiment after a vacuum-pump problem, a repair the account says took five astronauts and two days. One volunteered act in one secondary account, so a modest score at low confidence.
      proactive_agency: [55, 0.42, "d", "A"],
      // [src_kc_nasabio, src_kc_uta | 1982-1998 | NASA biography + university tribute] The NASA biography records three degrees in aerospace engineering from 1982 to 1988, research and company roles in aerodynamics, a flight-instructor certificate with airplane and glider ratings, several commercial pilot licences and aerobatic flying, and later work as lead of the Astronaut Office's crew systems and habitability section. A long record of formal skill-building is documented; the UTA tribute's praise for striving to excel is retrospective and admiring, so it adds little confidence.
      mastery_orientation: [60, 0.42, "d", "A"],
    },
