    sources: [{ id: "src_booth_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "William Booth (Q309882) -- verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-10-24): English label 'William Booth', description 'British Methodist preacher (1829-1912)', born 1829-04-10, died 1912-08-20, citizenship United Kingdom, Korean label '윌리엄 부스', P18 image 'William-Booth-c1900.jpg'. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q309882" }, { id: "src_booth_eb1911", kind: "archive", title: "Encyclopaedia Britannica, 11th edition (1911), entry 'Booth, William' (Wikisource transcription), read in full: founder and 'general' of the Salvation Army; Wesleyan local preacher, then Methodist New Connexion minister, left in 1861; organised the East London Christian Mission in 1864 which became the Salvation Army in 1878; methods (open-air meetings, popular tunes, converts as daily witnesses); quasi-military 'Orders and Regulations'; early violent opposition and fines and imprisonment of followers; spread to the United States (1880), Australia (1881) and beyond; In Darkest England and the Way Out (1890) with its ten expedients, money liberally subscribed; later royal and civic recognition. CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE. Contemporary reference entry written in his lifetime, sympathetic.", url: "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica/Booth,_William" }, { id: "src_booth_sa", kind: "institution", title: "The Salvation Army UK and Ireland, 'Our history' page (salvationarmy.org.uk), read in full: the movement began in East London in 1865 when William and Catherine Booth took preaching to the streets, the Christian Mission with Booth as General-Superintendent, the 1878 name change and quasi-military structure, the 'soup, soap and salvation' practical doctrine, and the range of social services. Self-description by the organisation he founded; admiring and used only for chronology and the organisation's own account of its work.", url: "https://www.salvationarmy.org.uk/about-us/our-history" }, { id: "src_booth_edwards", kind: "biography", title: "Bruce L. Edwards, 'William Booth' (EBSCO Research Starters, History, 2024), read in full: early life and conversion, the 1865 Whitechapel mission, the military metaphor (general, ranks, uniforms, flags, band), the 1878-1890 growth, the statement that his 'increasingly rigid, authoritarian manner' alienated many within the ranks and that his public power alarmed outsiders, the 1890 manifesto and the controversy and charges of socialism, Huxley as a secularist counterpoint, and an annotated bibliography that characterises Begbie as written as a tribute and Hattersley as more candid about the Booths. A modern encyclopaedic overview that is balanced and names criticism.", url: "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/william-booth/" }, { id: "src_booth_begbie", kind: "biography", title: "Harold Begbie, 'The Life of General William Booth, the Founder of the Salvation Army' (Macmillan, New York, 1920, two volumes), full-text scans read selectively on the passages cited: the Mile End Waste open-air preaching and a convert's account of him; his 1855-era letters; the origins of the social scheme; the 1890 manifesto and its conflicting reception; the 1892 Committee of Inquiry into the Darkest England fund (about 129,000 pounds subscribed by 1892, doubts about personal benefit and accounts, the committee finding the funds had been spent only on the declared objects); quoted contemporary critics (Randall Davidson on his autocratic control; Huxley's attack; W. T. Stead's reproof to Railton); the motor-car campaigns of his old age. Begbie was a devoted admirer (EBSCO calls the work a tribute); the book is used for documented episodes and quoted documents, not for its praise, and no family or health passages are used.", url: "https://archive.org/details/lifeofgeneralwil01begb" }, { id: "src_booth_dnb", kind: "biography", title: "Edward Harold Begbie, 'Booth, William', Dictionary of National Biography, 1927 supplement (Wikisource transcription), read in full: poverty and pawnbroker apprenticeship, 1844 conversion, break with the Methodist New Connexion in 1861 'rather than submit to the authority of his church', the 1878 'volunteer army' to 'salvation army' change and the military titles he at first resisted, the 1890 book largely written by W. T. Stead, Huxley's attack, and a markedly more critical character assessment than Begbie's biography (a 'timorous administrator' using his autocracy to safeguard spiritual work, hostile to science and sport, willing to court wealthy donors, with Bramwell the real organiser). Same author as the biography so not fully independent; the passages on ancestry and speculation about racial character are deliberately NOT used.", url: "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1927_supplement/Booth,_William" }, { id: "src_booth_foote", kind: "archive", title: "G. W. Foote, 'Salvation Syrup; or, Light on Darkest England' (1891 freethinker's pamphlet, Project Gutenberg eBook 39120), read in the passages on organisation and finance: a secularist critic's charge that Booth held 'despotic' unchecked control over a body of followers whose daily lives were regulated, that the scheme's proposed farm and over-sea colonies would be despotic rather than co-operative, and that W. T. Stead wrote much of the manifesto. Adversarial, partisan and of its time; used only as the contemporary counter-voice on authority and governance, not for factual claims about his character.", url: "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/39120" }, { id: "src_booth_commons", kind: "institution", title: "Wikimedia Commons file page 'Gen. Booth LCCN2014685085.jpg' (wikitext and image record), checked for the portrait: Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs Division, George Grantham Bain Collection glass negative, publisher Bain News Service, photographer not credited, no date recorded; the file page quotes the Library's statement of no known restrictions on publication. Original 4235 x 5854. Not used for any scoring.", url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gen._Booth_LCCN2014685085.jpg" }],
